{"name":"Star Dust","hex":"#f9f3dd","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"star","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any small, natural and bright dot in the sky, most visible in the night or twilight sky. This sense includes the planets, but it is now sometimes used in exclusion of them due to influence from the technical astronomical sense.","A planet thought to influence one's fate; (figuratively) fate or luck."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To appear as a featured performer or headliner, especially in an entertainment program.","To feature (a performer or a headliner), especially in a movie or an entertainment program."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/star"},{"term":"dust","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fine particles.","The act of cleaning by dusting."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove dust from.","To remove dust; to clean by removing dust."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dust"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cosmic dust","blocks":[{"text":"Cosmic dust – also called extraterrestrial dust, space dust, or star dust – is dust that occurs in outer space or has fallen onto Earth. Most cosmic dust particles measure between a few molecules and 0.1 mm (100 μm), such as micrometeoroids and meteoroids. Cosmic dust can be further distinguished by its astronomical location: intergalactic dust, interstellar dust, interplanetary dust, and circumplanetary dust. Information on the nature of dust in locations beyond the Solar System is mostly obtained by methods of observational astronomy, such as photometry, polarimetry and infrared spectroscopy. Direct methods are also available for the collection and study of space dust within the Solar System. For example, the Stardust spacecraft collected cometary dust, and also detected some particles of probable interstellar origin, returning samples to Earth in 2006."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg/330px-Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_dust","wikidata":"Q193384"},{"title":"Star Dust (Pat Boone album)","blocks":[{"text":"Star Dust is the sixth studio album by Pat Boone, released in 1958 on Dot Records."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/af/Star_Dust_%28Pat_Boone_album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Dust_(Pat_Boone_album)","wikidata":"Q108860776"},{"title":"Star Dust (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Star Dust is a 1940 American comedy drama film directed by Walter Lang and starring Linda Darnell and John Payne, Roland Young and Charlotte Greenwood."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ad/Star_Dust_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Dust_(film)","wikidata":"Q1056936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg/500px-Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg/500px-Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Porous chondrite dust particle","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APorous_chondriteIDP.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_dust_formation_around_a_supernova_explosion.jpg/500px-Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_dust_formation_around_a_supernova_explosion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_dust_formation_around_a_supernova_explosion.jpg/1280px-Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of